# ============================================================
# Anima Companion — Environment Variables Example
# Copy to .env (local) or set in Vercel dashboard (production)
# ============================================================

# === DATABASE (Phase 3 — Neon PostgreSQL migration readiness) ===
#
# Two connection strings are required:
#
# 1. DATABASE_URL  — Neon pooled runtime endpoint (host with -pooler suffix).
#    Used by the Next.js runtime (Prisma Client) for all app queries.
#    Get this from Neon Dashboard → Project → Connection Details → "Pooled connection".
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@ep-xxxx-pooler.REGION.aws.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=require"

# 2. DIRECT_URL — Neon direct endpoint (host WITHOUT -pooler suffix).
#    Used by `prisma migrate`, `prisma db push`, `prisma studio`, `prisma introspect`,
#    `prisma validate`, `pg_dump`/`psql restore`, and any admin tooling that needs
#    a long-lived connection.
#    Get this from Neon Dashboard → Project → Connection Details → "Direct connection".
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@ep-xxxx.REGION.aws.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=require"

# Notes:
#   - .env is gitignored and NOT tracked in git (untracked via `git rm --cached`).
#   - Never commit real connection strings. The values above use placeholders.
#   - Production secrets are set in Coolify (or Vercel) project env vars, not in this file.
#   - If you ever paste a real URL here by mistake, rotate the password in the
#     provider dashboard immediately and rewrite history.
#   - Neon pooler defensive params (pgbouncer, connection_limit, pool_timeout,
#     prepared_statements) are NOT set by default. Only add them if runtime
#     testing shows actual need (e.g. "prepared statement does not exist" errors
#     under load) — don't pre-bake defensive config that may not be required.
#   - Local dev: set both vars to a Neon dev branch (or local Docker postgres)
#     in .env (not .env.example). SQLite `file:` URLs are NOT supported because
#     prisma/schema.prisma uses provider="postgresql" (keeps dev/prod parity).
#   - Migration source (Supabase, paused): to dump the existing Supabase
#     production database, the owner provides the Supabase direct connection
#     string out-of-band. It must never be committed to the repo or written
#     into chat logs.

# === AUTH ===
# Random secret for signing session cookies (7-day expiry, HMAC-SHA-256).
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
#
# IMPORTANT: AUTH_SECRET MUST be set in every production deployment.
# src/lib/auth.ts throws at module-load time when NODE_ENV === 'production'
# and AUTH_SECRET is missing — the server will refuse to start rather than
# fall back to a known dev secret. Set this in your deployment platform
# (Coolify / Vercel project env vars) BEFORE promoting a build.
# AUTH_SECRET is server-only; never prefix it with NEXT_PUBLIC_.
#
# Account Recovery & Verification V2: AUTH_SECRET is ALSO used as the
# HMAC pepper for OTP code hashes (src/lib/otp.ts). The 6-digit OTP code
# space is only 10^6, so a plain SHA-256 hash would be brute-forceable
# in microseconds if the DB leaked. By HMAC-peppering with AUTH_SECRET,
# an attacker must know AUTH_SECRET to mount even an offline brute force
# — and if they have AUTH_SECRET, the OTP is the least of our problems
# (they can forge session cookies directly). This is the same trust
# boundary as the session-cookie signing key, so reusing AUTH_SECRET is
# correct and intentional. Do NOT introduce a separate OTP_SECRET.
AUTH_SECRET="generate-a-random-32-hex-string-here"

# === NODE ENV (auto-set by Vercel) ===
# NODE_ENV="production"  # Vercel sets this automatically

# === SEO — Canonical & Indexing (Phase 1.1) ===
# Official canonical production domain. MUST be https://animacompanion.id on
# every deployment (production AND staging AND local). This is the absolute
# origin used for canonical URLs, OG URLs, JSON-LD `url` fields, the sitemap,
# and robots.txt host directive. Never set this to a staging/preview/Vercel/
# Coolify/VPS-IP URL — staging is excluded from indexing via ALLOW_INDEXING
# below, NOT by changing the canonical.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL="https://animacompanion.id"

# Whether this deployment may be indexed by search engines.
#   "true"  → production only (https://animacompanion.id)
#   "false" (or unset) → staging / preview / local / any non-production
#
# When this is NOT "true", the app forces noindex,nofollow sitewide (in root
# metadata) AND robots.txt disallows all crawling AND the sitemap is not
# advertised. Canonical URLs still point to https://animacompanion.id.
#
# ⚠️  CRITICAL — NEXT_PUBLIC_* vars are inlined at BUILD time, not runtime.
# This means the value present when `next build` runs is what gets baked
# into the static HTML. Set this at build time, not at server-start time:
#
#   # Staging build (default safe — non-indexable):
#   bun run build
#   # or explicit:
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_ALLOW_INDEXING=false bun run build
#
#   # Production build (indexable — only on https://animacompanion.id):
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_ALLOW_INDEXING=true bun run build
#
# The safe default is `false` — if the var is missing or wrongly set on a
# staging/preview build, that deployment will simply not be indexed, which
# is always recoverable. The reverse (accidentally indexing staging) is not.
NEXT_PUBLIC_ALLOW_INDEXING="false"

# === CLOUDINARY (admin product image uploads) ===
#
# Cloudinary is used ONLY for new product images uploaded from the Admin UI
# (admin form's "Upload Foto" button). Existing local images under
# /public/products/<slug>/*.webp are kept as-is and continue to work — the
# two URL types are interchangeable in the ProductImage.url column.
#
# The browser uploads image bytes DIRECTLY to Cloudinary via signed-upload.
# Our server only generates the signature using the API_SECRET; the secret
# itself never reaches the browser. See src/lib/cloudinary.ts.
#
# Required to enable admin uploads:
CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME=""
CLOUDINARY_API_KEY=""
# Server-only — NEVER expose via NEXT_PUBLIC_*:
CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET=""
# If left empty, the Admin upload UI shows "Cloudinary belum dikonfigurasi"
# and the manual URL paste fallback remains available.
#
# New uploads land in the Cloudinary folder: anima/products/


# === VERIFIED IDENTITY V1 — GOOGLE OAUTH ===
#
# Google Sign-In is OPTIONAL. If these env vars are NOT set, the Google
# button on the login page is hidden and /api/auth/google returns 503.
# We do NOT fake a Google login when it's unconfigured.
#
# To enable:
#   1. Create an OAuth 2.0 Client ID in Google Cloud Console
#      (APIs & Services → Credentials → Create Credentials → OAuth client ID)
#   2. Add the canonical redirect URI to "Authorized redirect URIs":
#        https://animacompanion.id/api/auth/google/callback
#      (and http://localhost:3000/api/auth/google/callback for dev)
#   3. Copy the Client ID + Client Secret below.
#   4. Restart the app.
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=""
# Server-only — NEVER expose via NEXT_PUBLIC_:
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=""

# === VERIFIED IDENTITY V1 — EMAIL DELIVERY ===
#
# Email delivery is used to send email-verification links to password users.
# Google users do not need this — their email is verified by Google.
#
# Adapter selection (lowercase):
#   dev (default)  — logs emails to server stdout (dev only). In production
#                    this adapter logs a CONFIG-MISSING error and DOES NOT
#                    send — forces the operator to wire a real provider.
#   brevo          — RECOMMENDED production provider. Sends via the Brevo
#                    Transactional Email API (POST https://api.brevo.com/v3/
#                    smtp/email) using native server-side fetch. Requires
#                    BREVO_API_KEY + EMAIL_FROM + EMAIL_FROM_NAME (see below).
#   resend         — Compatibility provider. Sends via Resend (resend.com).
#                    Requires RESEND_API_KEY + EMAIL_FROM (see below).
#   sendgrid       — V2 placeholder (not yet implemented; throws NOT_IMPLEMENTED)
#   ses            — V2 placeholder (not yet implemented; throws NOT_IMPLEMENTED)
#   smtp           — V2 placeholder (not yet implemented; throws NOT_IMPLEMENTED)
#
# For local dev, leave EMAIL_PROVIDER unset (defaults to "dev"). The
# verification URL is printed to the server console so you can paste it
# into a browser.
#
# For PRODUCTION, set EMAIL_PROVIDER=brevo and the three vars below. The
# BrevoEmailAdapter constructor THROWS if BREVO_API_KEY or EMAIL_FROM is
# missing/empty — the server will refuse to start rather than silently
# fake-send emails.
EMAIL_PROVIDER="dev"

# === BREVO (recommended production email provider) ===
#
# Required ONLY when EMAIL_PROVIDER=brevo. Ignored otherwise.
#
# Get BREVO_API_KEY from the Brevo dashboard (SMTP & API → API Keys →
# Generate a new key with "Transactional email" scope). Server-only —
# NEVER expose via NEXT_PUBLIC_*. NEVER commit a real key to this file or
# to git; set it in the deployment platform env vars (Coolify / Vercel)
# only.
BREVO_API_KEY=""

# The From: email address on every transactional email. Must be on a
# domain verified in the Brevo dashboard (Senders & IP → Senders → Add a
# Sender or Domains → Add a domain → follow DNS instructions).
# Example for production: noreply@animacompanion.id
EMAIL_FROM=""

# The From: display name shown in recipients' inboxes. Improves
# deliverability and open rates. Optional but strongly recommended — if
# unset, the adapter defaults to "Anima Companion".
EMAIL_FROM_NAME="Anima Companion"

# === RESEND (compatibility email provider) ===
#
# Required ONLY when EMAIL_PROVIDER=resend. Ignored otherwise.
#
# Get RESEND_API_KEY from the Resend dashboard (API Keys → Create API Key).
# Server-only — NEVER expose via NEXT_PUBLIC_*. NEVER commit a real key to
# this file or to git; set it in the deployment platform env vars
# (Coolify / Vercel) only.
RESEND_API_KEY=""

# When EMAIL_PROVIDER=resend, the From: address must use the Resend
# "Brand Name <localpart@verified-domain>" format (Resend accepts the
# display name inline). When EMAIL_PROVIDER=brevo, the display name is
# read from EMAIL_FROM_NAME instead and EMAIL_FROM must be the bare
# email address only.
#
# Example for Resend production:
#   EMAIL_FROM="Anima Companion <noreply@animacompanion.id>"
# Example for Brevo production:
#   EMAIL_FROM="noreply@animacompanion.id"
#   EMAIL_FROM_NAME="Anima Companion"

# === ADMIN REALM V1 — DEVELOPER BOOTSTRAP ===
#
# The internal admin realm (AdminUser table) is SEPARATE from the customer
# realm (User table). Admins sign in at /admin/login with username + password
# via the `anima_admin_session` cookie — no Google, no OTP, no public
# registration, no forgot-password.
#
# DEVELOPER is the highest privilege level. There is NO public API to create
# a DEVELOPER — they can ONLY be bootstrapped from these env vars via
# `prisma/seed.ts` (or directly via Prisma Studio / psql).
#
# BOOTSTRAP CONTRACT (enforced by prisma/seed.ts):
#   - Idempotent: if an AdminUser with DEVELOPER_USERNAME already exists,
#     the seed DOES NOT overwrite the password, role, or any other field.
#     Re-running the seed is safe — it only creates the developer on the
#     FIRST run.
#   - First-run-only: these env vars are read ONLY when no AdminUser with
#     DEVELOPER_USERNAME exists. After that, the developer manages their
#     own password via /admin/change-password and manages other admins via
#     the developer-only "Setting User Admin" UI.
#   - Hard-disabled demo in production: like the customer demo accounts,
#     demo admin users (admin@anima.id/admin123) are NEVER created when
#     NODE_ENV=production. The DEVELOPER env vars are the ONLY way to
#     bootstrap the first admin in production.
#   - MUST_CHANGE_PASSWORD: the bootstrap developer is created with
#     mustChangePassword=false (the operator chose the password), so they
#     can sign in directly. ADMINS created by the developer via the UI
#     are created with mustChangePassword=true and MUST change on first
#     login.
#
# These are server-only — NEVER prefix with NEXT_PUBLIC_. NEVER commit a
# real password to this file or to git; set them in the deployment platform
# env vars (Coolify / Vercel) ONLY on the FIRST deploy, then unset after
# the seed has run (the developer row persists in the DB).
#
# Choose a strong, unique password for the developer account — it has
# full bypass on every permission check.
DEVELOPER_USERNAME=""
DEVELOPER_PASSWORD=""
DEVELOPER_DISPLAY_NAME="Developer"
